1
00:00:00,670 --> 00:00:03,702
Here's a ticket asking
the payment service to

2
00:00:03,702 --> 00:00:06,733
retry failed transactions
exactly five times.

3
00:00:06,733 --> 00:00:11,449
Two clicks away, the team's accepted
decision says no more than three.

4
00:00:11,449 --> 00:00:13,470
Nobody notices until it ships.

5
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:20,084
Tether is a Forge app
for Jira and Confluence.

6
00:00:20,084 --> 00:00:23,461
It checks a ticket's technical
requirements against the

7
00:00:23,461 --> 00:00:27,820
decisions your team already recorded,
and shows you the exact evidence.

8
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:37,275
To make this easy to test, an admin grants
access once, then clicks Load demo data.

9
00:00:37,275 --> 00:00:40,039
In about a minute, Tether creates a demo

10
00:00:40,039 --> 00:00:43,632
project, a decision space
and thirty sample tickets.

11
00:00:43,632 --> 00:00:45,290
We've sped that part up.

12
00:00:48,660 --> 00:00:51,822
On PAY-1, I click Check
implementation context.

13
00:00:51,822 --> 00:00:56,060
The check runs in a background
queue, so I've trimmed the wait.

14
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:01,588
Conflict. On the left, the
sentence from the ticket.

15
00:01:01,588 --> 00:01:06,283
On the right, the sentence from the
decision, with a link to the page.

16
00:01:06,283 --> 00:01:10,240
Both quotes are checked word
for word against their source.

17
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:14,821
It also tells you what
it didn't check: one

18
00:01:14,821 --> 00:01:17,584
of two requirements had
no matching decision.

19
00:01:17,584 --> 00:01:19,918
And it shows why this decision counts.

20
00:01:19,918 --> 00:01:24,770
It's accepted, it applies to the payment
service, and it replaced an older one.

21
00:01:27,530 --> 00:01:30,137
Not every different number is a conflict.

22
00:01:30,137 --> 00:01:34,270
Four attempts, including the first,
is the same as three retries.

23
00:01:34,270 --> 00:01:37,640
Tether does that arithmetic
in code, not in a prompt.

24
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:42,810
When the counting is unclear, it says
Needs review instead of guessing.

25
00:01:43,990 --> 00:01:48,089
And when part of a ticket has no
decision behind it, it says so.

26
00:01:48,089 --> 00:01:51,100
Insufficient evidence is
never shown as a pass.

27
00:01:53,540 --> 00:01:56,385
Tether never edits the
ticket or the decision.

28
00:01:56,385 --> 00:01:59,973
If you want to follow up, it
drafts a clarification issue.

29
00:01:59,973 --> 00:02:03,003
You review it, change what
you like, and confirm.

30
00:02:03,003 --> 00:02:05,230
It's created as you, linked to both.

31
00:02:09,550 --> 00:02:10,628
Decisions change.

32
00:02:10,628 --> 00:02:15,196
When someone edits the retry decision,
the next time anyone opens PAY-1,

33
00:02:15,196 --> 00:02:20,080
Tether flags that a decision it relied
on has changed, and offers a re-check.

34
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:26,670
The same check runs inside Rovo.

35
00:02:26,670 --> 00:02:31,287
The Tether agent uses our actions
and a skill, so you can ask in chat.

36
00:02:31,287 --> 00:02:34,056
Here it reaches the same result for PAY-2:

37
00:02:34,056 --> 00:02:37,090
one requirement checked,
one with no decision.

38
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:44,094
Tether also exposes its tools over MCP.

39
00:02:44,094 --> 00:02:47,408
Here, Claude Code calls
check issue consistency and

40
00:02:47,408 --> 00:02:50,656
gets the same conflict,
with the quotes and links.

41
00:02:50,656 --> 00:02:53,580
This one is recorded on
our development site.

42
00:02:57,460 --> 00:03:01,759
To find decisions linked to a
ticket, Tether asks Teamwork Graph.

43
00:03:01,759 --> 00:03:04,933
Everything runs on Forge,
and the language model

44
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:08,240
calls use Forge LLMs, so
nothing leaves Atlassian.

45
00:03:11,470 --> 00:03:15,575
On our thirty team-written test
scenarios, all thirty pass.

46
00:03:15,575 --> 00:03:20,375
That shows it handles the cases we
designed, not real-world accuracy.

47
00:03:20,375 --> 00:03:22,810
Tether: tickets that stay tethered.
